<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mohegan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mohegan are an Algonquian-speaking Native American people of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut">Connecticut</a>. In the 1630s their sachem <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uncas">Uncas</a> broke away from the Pequot and allied with the English colonists, fighting beside them in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pequot-war">Pequot War</a> of 1637. They became the leading Native allies of Connecticut, and the Mohegan Tribe, federally recognised in 1994, has its reservation near Uncasville.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mohegan.jpg" alt="Mohegan" width="320" height="505" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Image courtesy of the Mohegan Tribe · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emma_Fielding_Baker_2.tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Mohegan are part of the Eastern Algonquian family of peoples and refer to themselves as the Wolf People. The name comes from an Algonquian term usually interpreted as People of the Wolf. They are distinct from the Mohican, or Mahican, who lived in the upper Hudson River Valley; English writers often confused the two, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> colonist Adriaen Block separated them in his writings as the Morhicans and the Mahicans.</p><p>The Mohegan and the Pequot are believed to share an ancestry in a coastal Algonquian-speaking population that moved south from the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudson-valley">Hudson Valley</a> and other inland regions. Both spoke Mohegan-Pequot, which was mutually intelligible with the speech of the Montaukett and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narragansett-people">Narragansett</a>. According to oral tradition and colonial records, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uncas">Uncas</a> broke from the Pequot sachem <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sassacus">Sassacus</a> and founded an independent community on the Thames River, centred on the fortified village of Shantok. Mohegan oral history says that Uncas broke the arrows of peace with Sassacus, and presents the split as a return to older values; colonial writers often saw it as opportunism.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Traditional Mohegan society traced descent through the mother's line, and women held influence in family and community decisions. A council of elders and sachems led by consensus rather than command. Families moved between river valleys, coast and inland forest with the seasons and lived in wigwams, domed dwellings framed with saplings and covered with bark or woven mats. Women grew corn, beans and squash, the Three Sisters, while men hunted deer and turkey and fished with weirs, nets and spears.</p><p>Mohegan belief centres on balance with the natural world. The Creator is often called Mundo in English, and decisions are guided by their effect on the seventh generation to come. The Trail of Life describes a path reaching seven generations back and seven forward. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/green-corn-ceremony">Green Corn Ceremony</a>, a harvest observance, was revived in the 20th century by Emma Fielding Baker.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Mohegan language ceased to be spoken in daily conversation in the early 20th century, but the Mohegan do not regard it as extinct. Samson Occom, a minister born in 1723, used it in preaching, and Fidelia Hoscott Fielding kept four phonetic diaries of Mohegan words and phrases that now underpin its revival. Gladys Tantaquidgeon, an ethnobotanist who lived from 1899 to 2005, recorded Mohegan medicine and co-founded the Tantaquidgeon Museum. In 2022 Dartmouth College returned Occom's papers to the Mohegan Tribe. Mohegan identity is understood as cultural as well as legal, and many Mohegan live across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mohegan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Connecticut forces and their <b>Mohegan</b> and Narragansett allies set fire to the fortified village, killing some 400 to 700 Pequot, many of them women and children.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-26">26 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The new towns fought the Pequot War in 1637 alongside the <b>Mohegan</b>, destroying the Pequot Nation, and in 1639 adopted the Fundamental Orders as their frame of government.</blockquote><a href="/state/connecticut-colony">Connecticut Colony</a></li><li><blockquote>Their wealth from the wampum trade, and their alliance with the Dutch, made them targets: the Dutch killed Tatobem in 1633 or 1634, and rival sachem Uncas broke away to lead the <b>Mohegan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/pequot-nation">Pequot Nation</a></li></ul></section>
